THE ART AND SCIENCE OF WEB DESIGN by Jeffrey Veen

2000. New Riders. Paperback.

This is a book I've owned for years (since 2003) and read the first
chapter or two a few times; but now in 2026 (a full quarter of a
century after its publication, an eternity in human time let alone
Internet time) I've read it, cover-to-cover.

This is a book about the Why? of web design, not about the How?. It's
the best explanation of a web designer's thought process; I really
feel that I know how a good web designer thinks having read the whole
book.

The book itself is beautifully coloured; originally it seems a bit
garish, like Wired magazine, but when you get used to it the colours
are exciting and appropriate. There's good use of cutaway sections and
bottom-of-page footer timelines. A few times I had to read to the next
subheading, then go back a page to read the cutaway page. The flow of
reading was slightly interrupted in doing this, but hey it's a book on
the web and the reader should be jumping around a bit in the reading,
even in a cover-to-cover linear reading.

After so much time a lot of the websites mentioned in the book are
defunct including hotwired.com (a digital yet separate form of Wired
Magazine) where Veen worked for over five years. One site stronger
than ever is netflix.com; here it is screenshotted and mentioned in
its original "borrow DVDs over the mail" incarnation. It's changed its
business model substantially since then. The chapter on websites
having to be fully optimised for speed since everyone will be on
modem's for another five years at least didn't bear out. I read a
great business story on how the Dot Com bubble crash allowed newer
companies to buy equipment at firesale prices, and the real innovation
happens later. Which is tough for the sharemarket investors who lose
their money when the companies collapse, but that's how innovation
works.

This is a fantastic reminder of the Internet as it was in 2000, the
great Dot Com wave is coming (companies are already using URLs on
billboards, Wired magazine is rocketing, people are starting to think
of targetted advertising not a common banner ad that is irrelevant to
a user and what he is searching for and doing on the site). It's also
a unintened hommage to the pre-Google internet and the great days of
the Portals (Yahoo, Lycos and the like) and the thinking behind those
sites; including a great introduction to Information Architecture.

There's a lot of whitespace on each page (or purplespace, or
greenspace in the two-page chapter introductions or the full-page
cutaways). Originally I thought this was to bump up the page count and
make the reader feel he was getting more content in a 272-page book,
but it's due to the paragraphs being thinner due to an old
typographical rule, from the print days, that I hadn't come across
before: "alphabet-and-a-half". The rule says that the most readable
paragraphs should have line lengths of about 40 characters per line
(26 letters in the alphabet, alphabet-and-a-half is 40 close enough).
Veen develops some nice CSS rules to apply that we can use on our own
web pages. I don't think alphabet-and-a-half is suitable to my own
writing (such as these book reviews) but I'll copy in Veen's words
here, give it a four-space indent with another two spaces for the
start of the paragraph. It's from page 120, about the relationship
between how something looks and how something works.

      Let's look at this relationship through
    developing the behaviour of a liquid page by manipulating its
    style. We'll start with an old typographic axiom: The most
    readable line lengths for a paragraph are "an alphabet and a
    half". This means that for maximum legibility in a chunk of
    content, each line in a paragraph should be roughly
    40 characters wide.

The second line looks jagged compared to the short first line but it
looks better in the book (though still a bit jagged). I've got an
old font/typography book "Looking Good in Print" in my garage that I
can neither bring myself to read (it's got to be so out of date) nor
bring myself to throw away (maybe, just maybe it may come in use
someday).

I'm a sucker for books beginning with the name "The Art of ". The most
famous of these is Knuth's The Art of Computer Programming: taocp.
The three words nicely abbreviate to "tao" so the series can also be
thought of as "Tao (of) Computer Programming". Veen's book certainly
deserves to be the art of, and there's enough technical information
and real-world stories to make it the science of as well. A quick
search of my book database shows the Eric Raymond classic
"The Art of UNIX Programming" and a whole slew of Kevin Mitnick hack
stories: The Art of Deception, The Art of Intrusion, The Art of
Invisibility. Not all of my "Art of" books will become classics; Art
of SEO will almost certainly stay unread (beautiful cover though) and
Art of Concurrency will be functionally used if I ever need it.

The Art and Science of Web Design is a beautiful book and a great
point-in-time history of the Internet in 2000.

